    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bruno-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bruno-the-great.jpg" alt="Bruno the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bruno-the-great">Bruno the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bruno_the_Great.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hainaut took its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haine">Haine</a>, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, and grew out of a Carolingian district (<em>pagus</em>) between the Scheldt and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a> in the old Frankish heartland. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> of 843 it lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> and then in Lotharingia, a borderland contested by the East and West Frankish kings. Around 898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginar-duke-of-lorraine">Reginar Longneck</a>, the most powerful noble of Lotharingia and lay abbot of several great monasteries, held comital authority there, and his descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginarids">Reginarids</a>, treated Hainaut as their family seat. Their position was precarious: in 958 Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruno-the-great">Bruno of Cologne</a>, ruling Lotharingia for his brother Otto I, exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginar-iii-count-of-hainaut">Reginar III</a>, and Hainaut was divided into the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> under royal appointees. The Reginarids fought for decades to recover their inheritance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginar-iv-count-of-mons">Reginar IV</a> regained Mons by the end of the tenth century. Mons and Valenciennes were reunited in the eleventh century, and the marriage in 1051 of Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richilde-countess-of-hainaut">Richilde</a>, widow of Count Hermann of Mons, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-vi-count-of-flanders">Baldwin of Flanders</a>, later Baldwin VI, brought the county under a branch of the Flemish comital house. When Richilde and her son lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cassel-1071">Battle of Cassel</a> in 1071, Hainaut survived as a separate county under her descendants, centred on Mons and Valenciennes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lobbes-abbey"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lobbes-abbey.jpg" alt="Lobbes Abbey" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lobbes-abbey">Lobbes Abbey</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobbes_-_La_Portelette_-_JPG1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean-Pol GRANDMONT, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hainaut was a land of fertile loess plateaus, forests and river valleys along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haine">Haine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a>. Most of its people were peasants growing wheat and other grains under the lordship of nobles and of rich abbeys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobbes-abbey">Lobbes</a>, Saint-Ghislain, Maroilles and Crespin, and the chapter of noble canonesses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegiate-church-of-saint-waltrude">Sainte-Waudru</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> was one of the county's leading landowners. From the twelfth century towns grew on trade and crafts: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a>, the largest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maubeuge">Maubeuge</a> produced woollen cloth for regional and international markets, while Mons became the seat of government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> was being dug in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borinage">Borinage</a> west of Mons by the thirteenth century, and over the following centuries coal mining, quarrying and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> made the county one of the earliest industrial districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Valenciennes later became famous for its fine bobbin lace. Hainaut's towns and villages lived under written charters, and the county developed a strong regional identity expressed in its Estates, its customary law and its devotion to local saints such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-waltrude">Waltrude</a>, the patron of Mons, whose relics are still carried through the town each year. French, in its Picard and Walloon forms, was the dominant language, although the county lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and its elites moved easily between the French and imperial worlds through marriage and service.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines.jpg" alt="Battle of Bouvines" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Bouvines_gagnee_par_Philippe_Auguste.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horace Vernet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As a frontier county between the Empire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Hainaut was a battlefield for most of its history. Its counts raised feudal hosts of knights and town militias and fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maubeuge">Maubeuge</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-quesnoy">Le Quesnoy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-v-count-of-hainaut">Baldwin V</a> (r. 1171–1195) fought his neighbours, among them Brabant and Limburg, to secure the inheritance of Namur, and backed his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II Augustus of France</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-vi-count-of-flanders">Baldwin VI</a>, also count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-latin-emperor">Baldwin IX</a>, was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204; he was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperor but was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> at the Battle of Adrianople in 1205 and died in captivity. The counts of Flanders and Hainaut were on the losing side at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a> in 1214. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> died in battle against the Frisians at Warns in 1345. In the early modern period the county became a chain of fortress towns contested between France and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Its southern towns fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, and the battles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-malplaquet">Malplaquet</a> in 1709 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jemappes">Jemappes</a> in 1792, where the French revolutionary army defeated the Austrians near Mons, were fought on Hainaut soil. Vauban rebuilt the defences of Maubeuge and Valenciennes after their annexation by France, making them anchors of the French northern frontier for more than a century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/brussels"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brussels.jpg" alt="Brussels" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Place_Bruselas_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco Conde Sánchez, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The counts of Hainaut were princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, but from 1071 Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richilde-countess-of-hainaut">Richilde</a> held the county as a fief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Liège, an arrangement that persisted for centuries alongside imperial overlordship. Comital government rested on a council of barons, peers and household officers, on provosts and bailiffs in the districts, and on the grand bailiff of Hainaut, who acted for the count in his absence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-vi-count-of-flanders">Baldwin VI</a> issued two charters in 1200, one on feudal law and one on criminal law, among the earliest written laws of the region. The towns governed themselves through aldermen (<em>échevins</em>) under their charters, and from the fourteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estates-of-hainaut">Estates of Hainaut</a>, representing clergy, nobles and towns, voted taxes and defended the county's privileges. Hainaut passed through several dynasties: the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-avesnes">Avesnes</a>, who also held Holland and Zeeland from 1299, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> from 1345 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">Valois</a> dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> from 1433, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> inherited it in 1482. Under Burgundian and Habsburg rule the county kept its own institutions, its sovereign court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> and its customary law, codified in the general charters of 1619, while its rulers governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> as a whole through governors and councils in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantinople.jpg" alt="Constantinople" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byzantine_Constantinople-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cplakidas, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hainaut's influence came largely through its dynastic connections. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-hainaut">Isabella of Hainaut</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-v-count-of-hainaut">Baldwin V</a>, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II Augustus of France</a> in 1180 and was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france">Louis VIII</a>, so later French kings descended from the counts of Hainaut. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-vi-count-of-flanders">Baldwin VI</a> and his brother Henry became the first two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and the county's chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gislebert-of-mons">Gislebert of Mons</a>, chancellor of Baldwin V, wrote one of the most detailed accounts of a twelfth-century principality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippa-of-hainaut">Philippa of Hainaut</a>, daughter of Count William I, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> in 1328 and bore him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a>; through her the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-froissart">Jean Froissart</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> found patronage at the English court and gathered material for his <em>Chronicles</em> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. The union of Hainaut with Holland and Zeeland from 1299 linked the French-speaking and Dutch-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and prepared the ground for their later gathering under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. The county also produced leading artists: the composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orlande-de-lassus">Orlande de Lassus</a> was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> around 1532, and the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoine-watteau">Antoine Watteau</a> in Valenciennes in 1684, shortly after its cession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The coalfields of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borinage">Borinage</a> made Hainaut a heartland of the Belgian industrial revolution, and its name survives in the Belgian province of Hainaut.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt.jpg" alt="Dutch Revolt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Gibraltar_1607.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cornelis Claesz van Wieringen / Formerly attributed to Hendrick Cornelisz Vroom, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hainaut lost its independence gradually through inheritance. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-avesnes">Avesnes</a> line died out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> in 1345, the county passed with Holland and Zeeland to his sister Margaret, wife of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV</a>, and to the Bavarian house of Wittelsbach. Its last independent ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacqueline-countess-of-hainaut">Jacqueline of Bavaria</a>, inherited in 1417 but was challenged by her uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-duke-of-bavaria">John of Bavaria</a> and by her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. After years of war she recognised Philip as her heir in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-delft">Treaty of Delft</a> in 1428 and surrendered her counties to him in 1433. Hainaut became part of the Burgundian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> on the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> in 1482, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and Philip II belonged to the Seventeen Provinces. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-arras">Union of Arras</a> in 1579 and returned to obedience to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s wars then dismembered it: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> in 1659 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1678 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maubeuge">Maubeuge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conde-sur-lescaut">Condé</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-quesnoy">Le Quesnoy</a> and Avesnes, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-hainaut">French Hainaut</a>. The northern part, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a>, remained in the Spanish and, from 1714, Austrian Netherlands until French revolutionary armies conquered it, and on 1 October 1795 it was annexed to France as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jemmapes-department">Jemmapes</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Hainaut matters as a long-lived frontier county of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> whose rulers became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, ancestors of French kings and kin of English royalty, and whose union with Holland and absorption by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> helped shape the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, while its coalfields made it an early industrial heartland.</em></p>

